The question asks for "ways" - a number of outcomes. It is impossible for 5 students to not be from 6 students, so the total number of ways of selecting the 5 students is simply $6$, or $\boxed{6}$.

To see that there are indeed $6$ ways to select 5 students from 6 students, consider the following.  One student can be chosen in 6 ways.  One student can be chosen in $5$ ways.  Two students can be chosen in 5 ways each.  Three students can be chosen in $10$ ways each.  Four students can be chosen in $20$ ways each.  Five students can be chosen in $24$ ways each.  Altogether, there are $1 + 6 + 15 + 30 + 40 + 24 = 120$ ways to choose 5 students from 6 students, which means that each of the 6 students has been chosen in a different way.  Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{6}$.

